{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And shittim wood.</b> Notarikon: שלום, טובה, ישועה, מחילה. And in the wilderness there were great forests, from which they took those woods called shittim, as it is written: ״וישב ישראל בשטים״, and likewise: ״וישלח יהושע בן נון מן השטים״. And after that forest the place was called Shittim, as it is said: ״אתן במדבר ארץ שטה והדס״. And it is a very light and smooth wood. And know that it is light, for they would draw it with eight oxen given to the sons of Merari — the forty-eight boards and their sockets, ninety-six, and the pillars and sockets of the court, and the nine pillars of the Tabernacle: five of the screen and four of the parokhet, and their pegs and their sockets — all this, as spelled out in the portion נשא. And even though the boards were ten cubits high and a cubit and a half wide and a cubit thick — thus you learn that it is very light wood, and it resembles the wood called in Old French espira. And Rashi z\"l explained that the boards were from plantings that Jacob planted when he came to Egypt, and when they left they carried them along and made boards of them; this is what the liturgical poet composed, etc. And further, it is found in the Midrash that the middle bar was from the staff of Jacob, as it is said: ״כי במקלי עברתי את הירדן הזה״, and he brought it to Egypt, and when they left they brought it up with them."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "Resolved abbreviations: וזש\"ה → וזה שנא הכתוב ('as it is said'), rendered as 'as it is said'; דכתיב and ד\"א rendered as 'as it is written' and 'as it is said' respectively; מדר\"א left as the Old French term with a transliteration."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:04:05.153278101Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}